nio: yield once to the runnable PID1 thread after bsd_init

CI 639 read PATH flags=0x150 (verified by enlarging the photo): the
init thread's initial wakeup ran (0x40) and thread_setrun() queued it
(0x10); the scheduler was entered (0x100) but never saw that thread
runnable (0x80 absent) and never committed it (0x20 absent).  With the
screen silent after "COMP done", the boot thread is running bsd_init to
completion without ever giving another thread the CPU.

The exec image is already installed in the init task, and the init
thread is sitting in task_wait_to_return with the return-wait flag
cleared, so it is ready to take its first trip to EL0.  Yield once at
the end of bsd_init (after the PATH summary and after all kernel-side
setup) instead of adding more read-only marks.

If the boot thread never returns from this yield, that is success:
launchd is running in userland.  No exec or SMP changes.


diff --git a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
index effd176..642bd10 100644
--- a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
@@ -1286,6 +1286,14 @@ bsd_init(void)
 		extern volatile unsigned int nio_user_path_flags;
 		printf("\nnio: PATH flags=%#x\n", (unsigned)nio_user_path_flags);
 	}
+	/* nio: 639 read PATH flags=0x150: the init thread was woken and made
+	 * runnable, but this boot thread never yielded after exec, so no
+	 * context switch ever happened.  Hand the CPU to it once here; the
+	 * exec image is already installed in the init task, so
+	 * task_wait_to_return -> thread_bootstrap_return should enter
+	 * launchd in EL0.  If this thread never comes back, that is the goal. */
+	printf("\nnio: YIELD to PID1 thread\n");
+	thread_yield_internal(1);
 }
 
 #if defined(XNU_OWNER_ROOT_AUDIT) && XNU_OWNER_ROOT_AUDIT
